@startuml registration
title Registration - the core scenario

autonumber
skinparam shadowing false

actor Client
participant "AuthController" as Ctl
participant "UserService" as Svc
participant "PersonClient" as PCl
participant "person-service" as Per
participant "KeycloakClient" as KCl
participant "Keycloak" as Kc

Client -> Ctl : POST /api/v1/auth/registration
activate Ctl
note right of Ctl : @Valid already checked\nformat and password confirmation
Ctl -> Svc : register
activate Svc

Svc -> PCl : createPerson
activate PCl
PCl -> Per : POST /api/v1/persons/registration
Per --> PCl : user_uid
PCl --> Svc : user_uid
deactivate PCl

group Domain user already exists past this line
  Svc -> KCl : createAccount
  activate KCl
  KCl -> Kc : POST /admin/realms/REALM/users\nno password, plus user_uid attribute
  Kc --> KCl : keycloak user id (Location header)
  KCl --> Svc : keycloak user id
  Svc -> KCl : resetUserPassword
  KCl -> Kc : PUT .../users/{id}/reset-password\ntemporary false
  Svc -> KCl : assignPlatformRole
  KCl -> Kc : GET .../roles/USER\nPOST .../users/{id}/role-mappings/realm
  Svc -> KCl : login
  KCl -> Kc : POST /realms/REALM/protocol/openid-connect/token
  Kc --> KCl : access, refresh, expiresIn
  KCl --> Svc : tokens
  deactivate KCl
end

Svc --> Ctl : TokenResponse
deactivate Svc
Ctl --> Client : 201 with tokens and user_uid
deactivate Ctl

note over Svc, Kc
  The order is deliberate: the Keycloak account is
  created only after the domain step succeeded, so
  no account is ever left without a domain user.

  Three admin calls, not one: Keycloak takes the
  password through its own endpoint and hands out no
  role on import. If the password or the role fails,
  rollbackAccountWithError deletes the account again -
  see keycloak-client.puml.

  The inverse is the open window. Any failure inside
  the group leaves an orphaned domain user, and the
  compensation policy applies - structured log, mark
  the registration inconsistent, return an error,
  hide nothing.
end note

@enduml
